---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
---
<Docs slug="formats">
  <p>
    LightPhotos opens the files straight from disk, in place. There is no
    conversion on the way in and no copy inside a library &mdash; what is in
    the folder is what you see.
  </p>

  <h2>What it opens</h2>
  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>RAW</strong> &mdash; Sony ARW, Canon CR2 / CR3, Nikon NEF,
      Fujifilm RAF, Panasonic RW2, Olympus / OM System ORF, Pentax PEF and
      DNG (Leica, Pentax, iPhone ProRAW, Pixel), rendered non-destructively
      like everything else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ordinary images</strong> &mdash; JPEG and TIFF on every
      platform, and <strong>HEIC in the macOS build only</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>HEIC is the platform-specific one.</strong> It is the format
    iPhones shoot, and on macOS it decodes through Apple's own ImageIO
    framework. The Windows, Linux and browser builds have no decoder for it
    &mdash; no pure-Rust HEIC decoder exists to build them on &mdash; so
    there, HEIC files are left out of the grid rather than opened
    half-working. Shooting or converting to JPEG keeps a folder readable on
    every platform.
  </p>
  <h2>Tested cameras</h2>
  <p>
    RAW support depends on the camera model, not just the brand. The macOS
    build decodes RAW through Apple's ImageIO, so it opens whatever
    <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/122870">Apple's camera list</a>
    covers. The Windows, Linux and browser builds use the open-source
    <a href="https://github.com/dnglab/dnglab">rawler</a> decoder instead.
    These are the files tested on both:
  </p>

  <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
    <table class="lp-compare">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Camera</th>
          <th scope="col">macOS</th>
          <th scope="col">Windows, Linux, browser</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Sony</strong> A7 IV</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Canon</strong> EOS R5</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Nikon</strong> D850, Z 6II, Z f, Z 8 (lossless)</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Nikon</strong> High Efficiency NEF (Z 8, Z 9)</td>
          <td>No</td>
          <td>No</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Fujifilm</strong> GFX100S, X-A5</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Fujifilm</strong> X-Trans: X-T4, X-T5, X-H2S, X100VI</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Browse only, from the camera's embedded preview</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Panasonic</strong> S5II, GH6</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>OM System</strong> OM-5 II</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>No</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Pentax</strong> K-r (PEF and DNG)</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Leica</strong> Q2 DNG</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>iPhone</strong> 12 Pro ProRAW, <strong>Pixel</strong> 8 Pro DNG</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Samsung</strong> Galaxy S23 Ultra DNG</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>No</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Nikon's High Efficiency compression is the gap on every platform; shoot
    lossless compressed NEF for now. Untested cameras from these brands will
    usually work, but a camera released after your copy of macOS or after the
    current rawler release may not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anything else in the folder is simply left out of the grid rather than
    raising an error. If your camera does not open,
    <a href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">the issue
    tracker</a> is where requests land.
  </p>

  <h2>What it writes</h2>
  <p>
    Three kinds of file, all of them next to your photos rather than off in an
    application support directory somewhere:
  </p>

  <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
    <table class="lp-compare">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Where it goes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Ratings and edits</strong></td>
          <td>Sidecar files in <code>.lightphotos/</code>, inside the folder you opened</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Thumbnail cache</strong></td>
          <td><code>.lightphotos/</code> too, so a folder carries its own thumbnails wherever it moves</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Exports</strong></td>
          <td>An <code>Exports/</code> folder inside the folder you opened, created on first export</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Your photos themselves are never moved, renamed or modified &mdash; the
    originals are read-only to LightPhotos. That is why
    <a href="/docs/export.html">exporting</a> produces new files instead of
    overwriting the old ones, and why moving a folder somewhere else keeps
    every edit with it.
  </p>
</Docs>
